A||||3||alpha||Ultimate Go-Kart almost complete!||||1||
Z||000000||alpha||05-14-2001||04:23 PM||||I have almost finished my go-kart project...I have been working on it off and on since January and I should have it out for its first tests this week. <P>It is powered by a 340cc 2 stroke from a snowmobile. A live rear axle is driven by the snowmobiles torque converter setup.<P>There is no suspension, though I am thinking adding a front suspension might be a good idea. The braking system is basically stock from the snowmobile, which should hopefully do okay by itself. It has a full cage for safety. I modified cyclocomputer for a speedometer/odometer.<P>I have a decent idea how fast this thing is going to go and am a little scared at the concept.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://fiero.dhs.org/fiero/sig.jpg">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu">douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||fropuff||05-14-2001||05:22 PM||||That sounds sweet!  I want to eventually do a street go kart w/ a 125cc engine and a 6-speed.  Keep us updated!<P>By the way, how fast do you figure?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000002||alpha||05-14-2001||06:27 PM||||When the engine and torque converter were attached to the snowmobile the snowmobile would go about 60mph.<P>I retained the stock gearing all the way to the rear wheels which have 13" tires... considerably larger then the drive roller on the snowmobile...<P>The amount of friction should be considerably less then the snowmobile as well.<P>I am scared to think how fast this thing will go. The top speed is met not when the engine runs out of revs, it is when the bearings burn up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||jerpro||05-16-2001||05:46 PM||||I have an oddyssey with a 410 snowmobile engine from an artic cat. .big paddle tires on the back and suspension all around.<BR>I am having some overheating problems with it though. I think I will have to get a bigger radiator.<BR>Without the air filters on I can get the ffron tires off the ground. (very scary the first time)<BR>I still have not dared to jump with it yet. I am afraid it will rool and the exhaust tips don't fit and are sticking out of the back about 10 inches.<BR>But man! You will love it once you get it on the track (or wherever) just make sure you wear a helmet and a five point harness. Trust me. you will be glad you had both.<BR>keep us updated!!!!<P><BR>btw. I have maxed out (better said chickened out) at 92. I think I am going to kill myself one of these days  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by jerpro (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
